SPU: an interpreted policy module (patch 7034) hands over to lifted code in verified resident spans, and back.

Games: A Crack in Time 1.20 (and Nexus, A4O, FFA): the igPm loader (0x230 bytes, runs once per SPU) stays interpreted (image -1),
but Insomniac's job kernel and its job blobs are streamed into local store from fixed EBOOT addresses (the RE notes section 31),
so they can be lifted at their load addresses and registered as verified streamed regions (tools/spu/lift_igjobs.py;
spu_overlay_register_verified_region: a GET from the registered EA to the registered LS address makes the span resident, the
first dispatch compares the lifted code bytes). Four hooks, all inert until such a region is registered and resident:
- spu_interp.c: an image -1 context rejoins lifted code only inside a bounded resident span (never a primary image: it has
  none, and the job blobs overwrite the loader's LS anyway), and stops at its caller's stop address like an image >= 0 one;
- spurs_policy.c: spurs_pm_interp_entry, when the interpreter returns still running (it reached such code), queues
  spu_indirect_branch so spu_run_with_halt's drain dispatches it;
- spu_channels.c: the dispatcher's KZ3 1069 interpreter fallback also covers image -1 policy contexts at any LS address, so a
  job the lift does not have (or whose bytes fail verification) runs interpreted, and it stops at the innermost open call
  bracket's return address (hop_ret_pc): the lifted kernel calls a job mid-function, and its host frame owns the
  continuation (without the stop the interpreter ran on through the kernel and the bracket's drain never returned);
- spu_drain.c: the drain's 1069 fallback (a lifted function returning into unlifted code) likewise covers image -1 policy
  contexts below LS 0x4000 (igPm's jobs load at 0x100).
Host test: port/rc/tests/run_igjob_rejoin_test.sh (a synthetic kernel and job lifted by lift_igjobs.py, linked with the
runtime: lifted kernel and job, an unlifted job, a job whose bytes fail verification, no resident kernel).

